This is where extract-msg from earlier comes in. The flow becomes: email file → pull out the attachment bytes → feed those bytes straight to pandas, no saving to disk required.

Step 1: Get the email as a file

From Outlook, drag the email out of your inbox into your project folder — it lands as a .msg file. That's it, that's the export.

Step 2: Install the parser (same venv, terminal):

python -m pip install extract-msg

Step 3: The script — save as parse_email.py:

python
import io
import extract_msg
import pandas as pd
from pathlib import Path

msg_file = Path(__file__).parent / "test_email.msg"
msg = extract_msg.Message(str(msg_file))

print(f"From:    {msg.sender}")
print(f"Subject: {msg.subject}")
print(f"Date:    {msg.date}")
print(f"Attachments found: {len(msg.attachments)}")

for att in msg.attachments:
    name = att.longFilename or att.shortFilename
    print(f"\n--- Attachment: {name} ---")

    if name and name.lower().endswith((".xlsx", ".xls")):
        # att.data is the raw bytes -- wrap in BytesIO so pandas
        # can read it like a file, no need to save to disk
        sheets = pd.read_excel(io.BytesIO(att.data), sheet_name=None)

        for sheet_name, df in sheets.items():
            print(f"\n=== Sheet: {sheet_name} — {df.shape[0]} rows x {df.shape[1]} cols ===")
            print(df.head(10))
            print("\nColumn types:")
            print(df.dtypes)
    else:
        print("(not an Excel file, skipping)")

Step 4: Email yourself the same test.xlsx, drag the email into the folder as test_email.msg, then:

python parse_email.py

The key move is io.BytesIO(att.data) — the attachment never touches disk, so no OneDrive locks, no Excel-has-it-open problems, no cleanup. The bytes go from inside the .msg directly into a DataFrame. From sheets onward, it's byte-for-byte the same as your working parse_test.py.

Two things you'll hit in the real world:

Inline tables vs. attachments — if someone pastes a table into the email body instead of attaching a file, that's the pd.read_html(msg.htmlBody) path from earlier. A production version of this script does both: check attachments, then scan the body for tables.
A folder of emails — the whole point eventually. Swap the single file for a loop:
python
for msg_file in Path(__file__).parent.glob("*.msg"):
    msg = extract_msg.Message(str(msg_file))
    ...

Drag twenty emails into the folder, run once, parse everything. That's the shape of a real ingestion job — and if you later get Graph API access to a mailbox, only Step 1 changes; everything from att.data down stays identical.

Try it with your test email and paste what comes out — the sender/subject lines printing correctly will confirm extract-msg handles your company's Outlook format before we trust it with anything bigger.
